{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1485597247,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyMDM3OTg4ODk=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1235","origin":"github"},"title":"`add_sibling` -\u003e `configure_sibling`?","message":"I was asking myself whether this would be a more appropriate name. Adding is mostly done through `create-sibling` commands, but reconfiguration will more frequently be done via this command. More issue aspects:\n\n- [ ] Git considers a remote to exist as soon as there is a single `remote.\u003cname\u003e.something` variable. We OTOH can do very little without at least a url.\n- [ ] In addition to the above, `add_sibling` requires `--force` to configure anything once a remote is already known. It would make sense IMHO to be able to reconfigure some aspects and still be able to have it test that the major setting -- the url -- stays unmodified.\n- [ ] GitPython will fail to fetch from a remote unless there is a `remote.\u003cname\u003e.fetch` config variable. At present we do not check that configuring a sibling always results in such a var to be set.","files":null}]}